import Shot from "../../../components/Shot.astro";
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  docKey="retouching"
  title="Retouching"
  description="How to fix a stacked result in Deptheos by painting from a source frame or another render: the brush settings, Only where colors match, undo and what is saved."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Retouching</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Every stacking method gets some places wrong: a soft patch where two depths cross, a halo along an edge,
      a hair that comes out twice. Retouching fixes them by painting pixels onto the result from an image that
      has them right, usually the one frame where that spot is sharp. The frames are already lined up with the
      result, so what you paint lands in place.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section id="open">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Opening Retouch</h2>
      <p>
        With a result on screen, click <em>Retouch</em> in the title bar, press <em>P</em>, or choose Stack ▸
        Retouch. The title bar then shows "Retouching #4" with Undo, Redo, Export and <em>Done</em>; the
        inspector holds the brush, and the list's header reads <em>Paint from · Click a frame</em>, with a
        <em>Frames | Renders</em> switch under it. Done, Esc
        or <em>P</em> again leaves Retouch. A result has to be saved as a render before it can be painted,
        which happens on its own a moment after a stack finishes. Retouch shows the render without its
        <a href="/docs/adjust/">adjustments</a>, as it was stacked; leaving Retouch draws them over the new
        paint.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot"><Shot name="paint-edge" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt="Retouch at 100%: source frames in the left list, Brush and Eraser sliders in the inspector, and the hornet result in the viewer." /><figcaption>Retouch: the source frame is chosen in the list, the brush in the inspector.</figcaption></figure>
  </section>

  <section id="sources">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What you can paint from</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>A source frame.</strong> Click it in the list, drag along the focus rail in the view bar, or
          step with ← and → while watching the spot. ⌥-click (Alt-click) on the picture picks the frame the
          result took that pixel from, and the rail's marker moves to it; a dot over a tick marks each frame
          you have painted from. Inside the brush ring you see the source, so you can tell before you paint
          whether it is sharp there. <em>Compare</em> in the view bar sets the source beside the painted
          result: its menu lists Painted, Split, Side by Side and Top and Bottom. The depth map
          (<em>M</em>) can be shown over the picture too, lit for the frame you are painting from; while it
          is, a drag moves around instead of painting, and a click on the map paints from the frame under it.
          Contribution is off in Retouch, where the source is shown as it is.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Another render of the same stack.</strong> Switch the list to <em>Renders</em> and click one,
          or pick it from the menu beside the render shown in the view bar, for example to paint a Depth render's clean edge onto a
          Pyramid result. It is lined up through a frame the two share and matched in brightness, and you paint
          from it as it was stacked, without its own retouching. A RAW stack's render and a JPEG stack's render
          can't be mixed.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The original result.</strong> <em>Eraser</em> (<em>E</em>) paints the result as it was stacked
          back over your strokes. <em>Brush</em> (<em>B</em>) goes back to the source you were using, and
          <em>X</em> flips between the two. Clicking the render's name at the start of the view bar makes the
          original the brush's source, so the brush paints it back as well.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>Hold <em>\</em> to see the whole source in place of the painted result.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="brush">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The brush</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="choose">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Setting</th><th scope="col">Starts at</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Size</th><td>30 px</td><td>The radius, in pixels of the picture. <em>[</em> and <em>]</em> make it smaller and larger.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Hardness</th><td>80%</td><td>How sharp the brush's edge is: high paints nearly evenly out to the ring, low fades gradually from the center, which blends a fix in softly. ⇧[ and ⇧] change it.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Opacity</th><td>100%</td><td>The most a stroke changes a pixel toward the source, however many times you go over it. Keys 1 to 0 set 10% to 100%.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Flow</th><td>100%</td><td>How much each pass adds, up to the opacity. Below 100% a stroke builds up as you go over a spot again.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>Only where colors match</h3>
      <p>
        This switch, off at first, makes the brush skip pixels where the source differs a lot from the result.
        Its <em>Within</em> slider (10% when turned on) sets how big a difference is allowed, taken per
        color channel relative to the brighter of the two, so it reads the same in shadows and highlights. Use
        it when painting near an edge: the sharp detail you want matches the result closely, while the halo or
        the shifted edge of a frame that is out of focus there does not, so it stays out.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="undo">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Undo, Reset and what is saved</h2>
      <p>
        ⌘Z undoes a stroke and ⇧⌘Z redoes it (Ctrl+Y also redoes on Windows and Linux), back through the strokes made
        since Deptheos started; the undo history is not kept after quitting. <em>Reset…</em>, under the brush, takes the render back to the
        stacked result and clears the undo history; it asks first, as it can't be undone.
      </p>
      <p>
        Painting is saved with the render from the first stroke, and is still there when you open the render
        again, after a restart too. Exports of the render include it. Grow and turntable sequences don't: they
        are made from the stack itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        On iPad, with the iPhone and iPad app (in beta), Apple Pencil pressure sets how much each stroke
        paints.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="ios">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>On iPhone and iPad</h2>
      <p>
        The iPhone and iPad app (in beta) keeps what the brush paints from along the bottom of the photo:
        the render you are retouching (a button that paints the original back when the stack has only one render,
        or a menu starting with Original and listing the stack's other renders); the focus rail, where a drag
        paints from the frame it rests on; the eye, which shows
        the source in place of the painted result (tap it, or touch and hold it while you look); and
        <em>Sharpest Frame</em>, after which a tap on the photo paints from the frame the result took that
        spot from. A tap with two fingers does the same.
      </p>
      <p>
        The view menu sets the source beside your work: Painted, Split, Side by Side or Top and Bottom, the
        two pictures moving and zooming together, with the brush ring on both. You paint on the painted side
        only. The same menu shows the depth map, lit for the frame you are painting from; a tap on the map
        paints from the frame under it. With a keyboard, the keys are the desktop's: ← and → step what the
        brush paints from, <em>[</em> and <em>]</em> size the brush, ⇧[ and ⇧] set its hardness, and
        <em>\</em> shows the source. A squeeze of Apple Pencil
        Pro can switch to the eraser or show the source, once per squeeze; choose which in Settings.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
